For SQL Server, the correct keyword for unique results is DISTINCT. So explain it to beginners as:

“When people say SELECT UNIQUE values in SQL Server, we normally use SELECT DISTINCT.”

Keep this video focused only on SELECT DISTINCT / unique values.

1. Select the database

Start with:

USE HealtchareDatabse;
GO

Explain:

“First, I am telling SQL Server that all my queries should run against our healthcare training database.”

2. Show the problem: duplicate values

Run:

SELECT CountryOfBirth
FROM dbo.Patient;
GO

Explain slowly:

“I am selecting only the CountryOfBirth column from the Patient table. Because many patients can be born in the same country, the same country appears many times.”

Example concept:

Canada
India
Canada
USA
India
Canada

Tell viewers:

“Sometimes I don't want every occurrence. I only want to know what different countries exist in my database.”

3. Introduce DISTINCT

Now run:

SELECT DISTINCT CountryOfBirth
FROM dbo.Patient;
GO

Explain:

SELECT          → Retrieve data
DISTINCT        → Return only different/unique values
CountryOfBirth  → Column I want
FROM            → Where the data comes from
dbo.Patient     → Patient table

Then say:

“DISTINCT removes duplicate values from the query result. It does not delete anything from the database.”

That last sentence is important.

4. Compare SELECT vs SELECT DISTINCT

Without DISTINCT:

SELECT CountryOfBirth
FROM dbo.Patient;
GO

With DISTINCT:

SELECT DISTINCT CountryOfBirth
FROM dbo.Patient;
GO

Explain:

“The first query can show Canada hundreds of times if hundreds of patients were born in Canada. The second query shows Canada only once.”

5. Try DISTINCT with Citizenship
SELECT DISTINCT Citizenship
FROM dbo.Patient;
GO

Explain:

“DISTINCT is not specific to countries. I can use it with other columns whenever I want to see the different values stored in that column.”

6. Try DISTINCT with AgeGroup
SELECT DISTINCT AgeGroup
FROM dbo.Patient;
GO

This is a good healthcare example.

You can explain:

“Instead of looking through 1,200 patient records, I can immediately see which age-group categories exist in my database.”

7. DISTINCT with multiple columns

Now introduce this carefully:

SELECT DISTINCT
    CountryOfBirth,
    Citizenship
FROM dbo.Patient;
GO

Explain:

“When DISTINCT is used with multiple columns, SQL looks at the complete combination of those columns.”

For example:

CountryOfBirth   Citizenship
--------------   -----------
India            Indian
India            Canadian
Canada           Canadian
Canada           Indian

All four can remain because each combination is different.

This is an excellent point for beginners:

“DISTINCT doesn't independently remove duplicates from each column. It removes duplicate combinations from the result.”

8. Make the output easier to read

Finish with:

SELECT DISTINCT CountryOfBirth
FROM dbo.Patient
ORDER BY CountryOfBirth;
GO

You don't need to deeply teach ORDER BY yet.

Just say:

“I am adding ORDER BY only to display the countries alphabetically. We will explain ORDER BY properly in another video.”

9. Final summary for the video

You can summarize verbally:

“SELECT retrieves data. DISTINCT tells SQL Server that I only want unique results. If Canada exists for 500 patients, SELECT can show Canada 500 times, but SELECT DISTINCT shows Canada only once. DISTINCT changes only the query result; it does not modify or delete patient records.”
